Unfortunately v2.7.0-beta-1 is behaving the same way ... But I succeeded to fix my problem just by disabling HTTP/3 protocol. Maybe this gives you a more specific hint regarding the problem?

@participant1 besides Caddy specific data... would you also need a test client certificate as part of the report ? (I can't provide you with my private one ...) 

Thanks anyway for your support!

My config (simplified):
```
{
        auto_https off
        https_port 443
        servers :443 {
                protocols h1 h2 h3
        }
}

(log_setup) {
        log {
                output stdout
                format json
                level DEBUG
        }
}


(missing_mTLS_cert) {
        @missing_mTLS_cert {
                expression {tls_client_subject} == null
        }
}

(ssl_setup) {
        import missing_mTLS_cert
        tls ./fullchain.cer ./server.key {
                alpn http/1.1
                client_auth {
                        mode verify_if_given
                        trusted_ca_cert_file ./client.crt
                }
        }
        forward_auth @missing_mTLS_cert 192.168.0.60:9091 {
                uri /api/verify?rd=https://authelia.server
                copy_headers Remote-User Remote-Groups Remote-Name Remote-Email
        }
        encode gzip
}

authelia.server {
        reverse_proxy 192.168.60.60:9091
}

server:443 {
        import log_setup
        import ssl_setup
        header subject {http.request.tls.client.subject}
        header issuer {http.request.tls.client.issuer}
        reverse_proxy http://192.168.0.60:1234
}
```
I was testing it against some simple website.
First request returns the headers correctly (probably because its still http1.1?) but afterwards with http3 requests i only see  placeholder strings "{http.request.tls.client....}" returned.

ALCON,

I am having the same issue where Caddy v2.6.4 only sometimes passes the client certificate to the backend server (in my case, Keycloak, but it shouldn't matter whether it's Keycloak, Authelia, or Authentik). Note that this issue only occurs if H3 is enabled _and_ `verify_if_given` is used. If `require_and_verify` is used, this issue doesn't occur. Below is my Caddyfile

```
{
    log {
        level debug
    }
    servers {
        protocols h1 h2 h3
    }
}

(client_auth) {
    tls {
        client_auth {
            mode verify_if_given
            trusted_ca_cert_file /certs/ca/root_ca.crt
            trusted_ca_cert_file /certs/ca/intermediate_ca.crt
        }
    }
}

keycloak.secure.local {
    import client_auth
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080 {
        header_up SSL_CLIENT_CERT {http.request.tls.client.certificate_pem}
    }
}
```

Attached [here](https://github.com/caddyserver/caddy/files/12208326/Caddy.Sanitized.Log) is my debug log. Note that I removed the following from the logs, do let me know if it is an issue:

1. The client certificate itself has been removed. All instances that contain certificate data have been replaced with "[CERT_DATA]"
2. Any references to the server certificate. I can't release this at this time, but if it's required I'll stand up a separate environment with releasable server certificate data.

Another difference is I cannot recreate the issue with curl. It seems to only occur with browsers (Firefox, Chrome) and only _sometimes_. I know this is unhelpful. I will also try the latest alpha version, as well, and comment here.

@participant3 I made some time to generate a root, sign a cert, and use them with your config to try to reproduce the issue.

A self-contained config based on yours:

```
{
	log {
		level debug
	}
}

(client_auth) {
	tls {
		client_auth {
			mode verify_if_given
			trusted_ca_cert_file /home/<user>/Downloads/rootCACert.pem
		}
	}
}

localhost {
	import client_auth
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080 {
		header_up SSL_CLIENT_CERT {http.request.tls.client.certificate_der_base64}
	}
}

:8080 {
	respond "Client cert: {header.SSL_CLIENT_CERT}"
}
```

Notice that I've changed the placeholder to `{http.request.tls.client.certificate_der_base64}` to get a value valid for headers. I also removed the `protocols` line because that is the default value anyway.

Then running your curl command:

```
$ curl --cert cert.crt --key cert.key https://localhost --next https://localhost
Client cert:  cert: {http.request.tls.client.certificate_der_base64}
```

This all looks as expected. The extra `Client cert: {http.request.tls.client.certificate_der_base64}` at the end is normal because of the `--next https://localhost` request which makes a second request without client auth.

If this is a faithful simplification of the actual issue then I'm not sure there is a problem in Caddy here. Maybe the client is not sending a TLS client certificate in @reporter's case? I dunno.

Hi folks, I got very good progress and I am able to consistently replicate the issue with Curl and Caddy 2.7.6. Thanks to @participant2 for linking to HTTP3 version of Curl in the thread [here](https://caddy.community/t/caddy-not-using-http3/19555/2). 

It seems the issue always occurs in HTTP3/QUIC. It does not occur over HTTP1 or HTTP2. Previously, I stated "[i]t seems to only occur with browsers (Firefox, Chrome) and only sometimes." This is false, wrong and absolute misinformation. The only reason why it occurs "sometimes" is because Firefox and Chrome tend to switch between HTTP2 and HTTP3 seemingly in a random fashion. I'm not quite sure what gets them to switch between the two, but I reckon that's out of scope of this issue.

I can confirm that this behavior is always consistent, given the variables below. 

Given the following Caddyfile:

```
{
    log {
        level debug
    }
}

(client_auth) {
    tls {
        dns cloudflare [SECRET REDACTED]
        client_auth {
            mode verify_if_given
            trusted_ca_cert_file /intermediate.crt
        }
    }
}

caddytest.homelab.memetichenry.com {
    import client_auth
    bind 10.12.180.18
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5000 {
        header_up tls-client-certificate {http.request.tls.client.certificate_der_base64}
        header_up soundness-check are-you-still-there
    }
}
```

and the following Docker Compose file:


```yml
services:
  caddy:
    build: Cloudflare_Caddy
    volumes:
      - ./Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:Z
      - ./certs/intermediate.crt:/intermediate.crt:Z
      - ./caddy_data:/data:Z
      - ./caddy_config:/config:Z
    network_mode: "host"
    restart: unless-stopped
```

Where "Cloudflare_Caddy" is simply Caddy with the official Cloudflare module, the following curl command:

```
curl -E ./client.crt --key ./client.key -vs -D/dev/stdout --http3 https://caddytest.homelab.memetichenry.com
```

Will always generate the following reverse proxy request, taken from the Caddy log:

```json
